From 0c39fad170911edb62f29f60147466abca7862e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jens Steube <jens.steube@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:02:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Prevent out of bounds device writes in RACF modes

Modes 14200 and 37500 read a memory factor from the 32 character RACF header and use it to determine a kernel loop bound and an index into racf_kdfaes_tmp->out, a buffer of 256 words. Mode 37500 did not validate the factor. Mode 14200 rejected values above 10 but accepted values from 1 through 4, which become 0 in the subsequent division. A factor of 000B makes mode 37500 write to out[256] through out[511], while 0001 makes mode 14200 index out[] at 8 * (0 - 1). Both cases can be triggered by a hash file.

Both parsers now reject memory factors outside the range 5 through 10. A factor of 10 fills the buffer exactly, while factors below 5 produce 0 after division. Mode 37500 now also performs the shift on an unsigned value, as mode 14200 already did, so the shift remains defined regardless of the header value.

The repetition factor from the same header was also unchecked in both modes. It determines two iteration counts, each one less than a multiple of the factor. A factor of 0 therefore produced 0xffffffff for both counts, and both parsers now reject it.

Both init2 kernels also return early when the memory factor is 0. That keeps the unconditional tail write at the end of each from indexing out[] at 8 * (0 - 1) if a future parser change ever allows 0 through.
---
 OpenCL/m14200-pure.cl      |  5 +++++
 OpenCL/m37500-pure.cl      |  5 +++++
 src/modules/module_14200.c | 10 ++++++++--
 src/modules/module_37500.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 tools/fuzz/fuzz_parse.dict |  1 +
 5 files changed, 31 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/OpenCL/m14200-pure.cl b/OpenCL/m14200-pure.cl
index fc2ce95ed9..86e77dd6df 100644
--- a/OpenCL/m14200-pure.cl
+++ b/OpenCL/m14200-pure.cl
@@ -590,6 +590,11 @@ KERNEL_FQ KERNEL_FA void m14200_init2 (KERN_ATTR_TMPS_ESALT (racf_kdfaes_tmp_t,
 
   if (gid >= GID_CNT) return;
 
+  // The parser rejects a mem_fac that divides to 0. The tail write at the end of this kernel
+  // would index out[] at 8 * (0 - 1) if one ever reached here.
+
+  if (esalt_bufs[DIGESTS_OFFSET_HOST].mem_fac == 0) return;
+
   tmps[gid].key[0] = tmps[gid].salt_buf[4];
   tmps[gid].key[1] = tmps[gid].salt_buf[5];
   tmps[gid].key[2] = tmps[gid].salt_buf[6];
diff --git a/OpenCL/m37500-pure.cl b/OpenCL/m37500-pure.cl
index 1070e3ae33..a77f5165e3 100644
--- a/OpenCL/m37500-pure.cl
+++ b/OpenCL/m37500-pure.cl
@@ -531,6 +531,11 @@ KERNEL_FQ KERNEL_FA void m37500_init2 (KERN_ATTR_TMPS_ESALT (racf_kdfaes_tmp_t,
 
   if (gid >= GID_CNT) return;
 
+  // The parser rejects a mem_fac that divides to 0. The tail write at the end of this kernel
+  // would index out[] at 8 * (0 - 1) if one ever reached here.
+
+  if (esalt_bufs[DIGESTS_OFFSET_HOST].mem_fac == 0) return;
+
   tmps[gid].key[0] = tmps[gid].salt_buf[4];
   tmps[gid].key[1] = tmps[gid].salt_buf[5];
   tmps[gid].key[2] = tmps[gid].salt_buf[6];
diff --git a/src/modules/module_14200.c b/src/modules/module_14200.c
index a203f59df8..e831ea7e21 100644
--- a/src/modules/module_14200.c
+++ b/src/modules/module_14200.c
@@ -259,9 +259,15 @@ int module_hash_decode (MAYBE_UNUSED const hashconfig_t *hashconfig, MAYBE_UNUSE
    */
   // mem_fac is four hex digits off the line, so it reaches 0xffff, and the shift below is then
   // undefined rather than large. The buffer it sizes is racf_kdfaes_tmp->out, u32[256], which is
-  // what a mem_fac of 10 fills, and a mem_fac of 0 would shift by -1.
+  // what a mem_fac of 10 fills. Below 5 the division gives 0, and the kernel then indexes out[]
+  // with 8 * (0 - 1).
 
-  if ((mem_fac < 1) || (mem_fac > 10)) return (PARSER_SALT_VALUE);
+  if ((mem_fac < 5) || (mem_fac > 10)) return (PARSER_SALT_VALUE);
+
+  // rep_fac comes off the same header and sets both iteration counts, each one less than a
+  // multiple of it, so a rep_fac of 0 makes them 0xffffffff.
+
+  if (rep_fac == 0) return (PARSER_SALT_ITERATION);
 
   racf_kdfaes->mem_fac = (2u << (mem_fac - 1)) / 32;
   /**
diff --git a/src/modules/module_37500.c b/src/modules/module_37500.c
index 0b19c3009b..37ed810782 100644
--- a/src/modules/module_37500.c
+++ b/src/modules/module_37500.c
@@ -248,7 +248,18 @@ int module_hash_decode (MAYBE_UNUSED const hashconfig_t *hashconfig, MAYBE_UNUSE
   rep_fac |= ((u32) hex_convert (header_pos[20+3]) <<  0);
   rep_fac |= ((u32) hex_convert (header_pos[20+2]) <<  4);
 
-  racf_kdfaes->mem_fac = (2 << (mem_fac - 1)) / 32;
+  // Both factors are four hex digits off the line and reach 0xffff. The buffer mem_fac sizes is
+  // racf_kdfaes_tmp->out, u32[256], which is what a mem_fac of 10 fills. Below 5 the division
+  // gives 0, and the kernel then indexes out[] with 8 * (0 - 1).
+
+  if ((mem_fac < 5) || (mem_fac > 10)) return (PARSER_SALT_VALUE);
+
+  // rep_fac sets both iteration counts, each one less than a multiple of it, so a rep_fac of 0
+  // makes them 0xffffffff.
+
+  if (rep_fac == 0) return (PARSER_SALT_ITERATION);
+
+  racf_kdfaes->mem_fac = (2u << (mem_fac - 1)) / 32;
   racf_kdfaes->rep_fac = rep_fac;
 
   // salt (save to racf_kdfaes->salt_buf and salt->salt_buf)
diff --git a/tools/fuzz/fuzz_parse.dict b/tools/fuzz/fuzz_parse.dict
index ee7443be8a..04a117e50b 100644
--- a/tools/fuzz/fuzz_parse.dict
+++ b/tools/fuzz/fuzz_parse.dict
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
 "$office$"
 "$RAR3$"
 "$7z$"
+"$racf-kdfaes$*"
 
 "0123456789abcdef"
 "deadbeef"
